  <p><em>This article was discussed <a href=3D"https://news.ycombinator.com=
/item?id=3D35758898">on Hacker News</a> and <a href=3D"https://old.reddit.c=
om/r/C_Programming/comments/133bjlp">on reddit</a>.</em></p>

<p>The major compilers have an <a href=3D"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/=
man1/gcc.1.html">enormous number of knobs</a>. Most are
highly specialized, but others are generally useful even if uncommon. For
warnings, the venerable <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge=
">-=EF=BB=BFWall -=EF=BB=BFWextra</code> is a good start, but
circumstances improve by tweaking this warning set. This article covers
high-hitting development-time options in GCC, Clang, and MSVC that ought
to get more consideration.</p>

<!--more-->

<p>There=E2=80=99s an irony that the more you use these options, the less u=
seful they
become. Given a reasonable workflow, they are a harsh mistress in a fast,
tight feedback loop quickly breaking the habits that cause warnings and
errors. It=E2=80=99s a kind of self-improvement, where eventually most find=
ings
will be false positives. With heuristics internalized, you will be able
spot the same issues just reading code =E2=80=94 a handy skill during code =
review.</p>

<h3 id=3D"static-warnings">Static warnings</h3>

<p>Traditionally, C and C++ compilers are by default conservative with
warnings. Unless configured otherwise, they only warn about the most
egregious issues where it=E2=80=99s highly confident. That=E2=80=99s too co=
nservative. For
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">gcc</code> and <code c=
lass=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">clang</code>, the first order=
 of business is turning on more warnings
with <strong><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-=EF=BB=
=BFWall</code></strong>. Despite the name, this doesn=E2=80=99t actually en=
able all
warnings. (<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">clang</code=
> has <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-=EF=BB=BFWevery=
thing</code> which does literally this, but
trust me, you don=E2=80=99t want it.) However, that still falls short, and =
you=E2=80=99re
better served enabling <em>extra</em> warnings on with <strong><code class=
=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-=EF=BB=BFWextra</code></strong>.=
</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>$ cc -Wall -Wextra ...
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>That should be the baseline on any new project, and closer to what these
compilers should do by default. Not using these means leaving value on the
table. If you come across such a project, there=E2=80=99s a good chance you=
 can
find bugs statically just by using this baseline. Some warnings only occur
at higher <a href=3D"https://www.openwall.com/lists/musl/2023/05/22/2/1">op=
timization levels</a>, so leave these on for your release
builds, too.</p>

<p>For MSVC, including <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"=
>clang-cl</code>, a similar baseline is <strong><code class=3D"language-pla=
intext highlighter-rouge">/W4</code></strong>. Though it
goes a bit far, warning about use of unary minus on unsigned types
(C4146), and sign conversions (C4245). If you=E2=80=99re <a href=3D"https:/=
/nullprogram.com/blog/2023/02/15/">using a CRT</a>, also
disable the bogus and irresponsible =E2=80=9Csecurity=E2=80=9D warnings. Pu=
tting it
together, the warning baseline becomes:</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>$ cl /W4 /wd4146 /wd4245 /D_CRT_SECURE_NO_=
WARNINGS ...
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>As for <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">gcc</code> a=
nd <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">clang</code>, I dis=
like unused parameter warnings, so I often
turn it off, at least while I=E2=80=99m working: <strong><code class=3D"lan=
guage-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-=EF=BB=BFWno-unused-parameter</code></s=
trong>.
Rarely is it a defect to not use a parameter. It=E2=80=99s common for a fun=
ction
to fit a fixed prototype but not need all its parameters (e.g. <code class=
=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">WinMain</code>).
Were it up to me, this would not be part of <code class=3D"language-plainte=
xt highlighter-rouge">-=EF=BB=BFWextra</code>.</p>

<p>I also dislike unused functions warnings: <strong><code class=3D"languag=
e-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-=EF=BB=BFWno-unused-function</code></strong=
>.
I can=E2=80=99t say this is wrong for the baseline since, in most cases, ul=
timately
I do want to know if there are unused functions, e.g. to be deleted. But
while I=E2=80=99m working it=E2=80=99s usually noise.</p>

<p>If I=E2=80=99m <a href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/blog/2017/03/01/">work=
ing with OpenMP</a>, I may also disable warnings about
unknown pragmas: <strong><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-roug=
e">-=EF=BB=BFWno-unknown-pragmas</code></strong>. One cool feature of
OpenMP is that the typical case gracefully degrades to single-threaded
behavior when not enabled. That is, compiling without <code class=3D"langua=
ge-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-=EF=BB=BFfopenmp</code>.
I=E2=80=99ll test both ways to ensure I get deterministic results, or just =
to ease
debugging, and I don=E2=80=99t want warnings when it=E2=80=99s disabled. It=
=E2=80=99s fine for the
baseline to have this warning, but sometimes it=E2=80=99s a poor match.</p>

<p>When working with single-precision floats, perhaps on games or graphics,
it=E2=80=99s easy to accidentally introduce promotion to double precision, =
which
can hurt performance. It could be neglecting an <code class=3D"language-pla=
intext highlighter-rouge">f</code> suffix on a constant
or using <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">sin</code> in=
stead of <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">sinf</code>. =
Use <strong><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-=EF=BB=BF=
Wdouble-promotion</code></strong> to
catch such mistakes. Honestly, this is important enough that it should go
into the baseline.</p>

<div class=3D"language-c highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre c=
lass=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"cp">#define PI 3.141592653589793
</span><span class=3D"kt">float</span> <span class=3D"n">degs</span> <span =
class=3D"o">=3D</span> <span class=3D"p">...;</span>
<span class=3D"kt">float</span> <span class=3D"n">rads</span> <span class=
=3D"o">=3D</span> <span class=3D"n">degs</span> <span class=3D"o">*</span> =
<span class=3D"n">PI</span> <span class=3D"o">/</span> <span class=3D"mi">1=
80</span><span class=3D"p">;</span>  <span class=3D"c1">// warns about prom=
otion</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>It can be awkward around variadic functions, particularly <code class=3D=
"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">printf</code>, which
cannot receive <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">float</=
code> arguments, and so implicitly converts. You=E2=80=99ll need
a explicit cast to disable the warning. I imagine this is the main reason
the warning is not part of <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-ro=
uge">-=EF=BB=BFWextra</code>.</p>

<div class=3D"language-c highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre c=
lass=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"kt">float</span> <span class=3D"n">=
x</span> <span class=3D"o">=3D</span> <span class=3D"p">...;</span>
<span class=3D"n">printf</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"s">=
"%.17g</span><span class=3D"se">\n</span><span class=3D"s">"</span><span cl=
ass=3D"p">,</span> <span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"kt">double</spa=
n><span class=3D"p">)</span><span class=3D"n">x</span><span class=3D"p">);<=
/span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Finally, an advanced option: <strong><code class=3D"language-plaintext h=
ighlighter-rouge">-=EF=BB=BFWconversion -Wno-sign-conversion</code></strong=
>.
It warns about implicit conversions that may result in data loss. Sign
conversions do not have data loss, the implicit conversions are useful,
and in my experience they=E2=80=99re not a source of defects, so I disable =
that
part using the second flag (like MSVC <code class=3D"language-plaintext hig=
hlighter-rouge">/wd4245</code>). The important warning
here is truncation of size values, warning about unsound uses of sizes and
subscripts. For example:</p>

<div class=3D"language-c highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre c=
lass=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"c1">// NOTE: would be declared/defi=
ned via windows.h</span>
<span class=3D"k">typedef</span> <span class=3D"kt">uint32_t</span> <span c=
lass=3D"n">DWORD</span><span class=3D"p">;</span>
<span class=3D"n">BOOL</span> <span class=3D"nf">WriteFile</span><span clas=
s=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">HANDLE</span><span class=3D"p">,</span> <=
span class=3D"k">const</span> <span class=3D"kt">void</span> <span class=3D=
"o">*</span><span class=3D"p">,</span> <span class=3D"n">DWORD</span><span =
class=3D"p">,</span> <span class=3D"n">DWORD</span> <span class=3D"o">*</sp=
an><span class=3D"p">,</span> <span class=3D"n">OVERLAPPED</span> <span cla=
ss=3D"o">*</span><span class=3D"p">);</span>

<span class=3D"kt">void</span> <span class=3D"nf">logmsg</span><span class=
=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"kt">char</span> <span class=3D"o">*</span><sp=
an class=3D"n">msg</span><span class=3D"p">,</span> <span class=3D"kt">size=
_t</span> <span class=3D"n">len</span><span class=3D"p">)</span>
<span class=3D"p">{</span>
    <span class=3D"n">HANDLE</span> <span class=3D"n">err</span> <span clas=
s=3D"o">=3D</span> <span class=3D"n">GetStdHandle</span><span class=3D"p">(=
</span><span class=3D"n">STD_ERROR_HANDLE</span><span class=3D"p">);</span>
    <span class=3D"n">DWORD</span> <span class=3D"n">out</span><span class=
=3D"p">;</span>
    <span class=3D"n">WriteFile</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=
=3D"n">err</span><span class=3D"p">,</span> <span class=3D"n">msg</span><sp=
an class=3D"p">,</span> <span class=3D"n">len</span><span class=3D"p">,</sp=
an> <span class=3D"o">&amp;</span><span class=3D"n">out</span><span class=
=3D"p">,</span> <span class=3D"mi">0</span><span class=3D"p">);</span>  <sp=
an class=3D"c1">// len truncation warning</span>
<span class=3D"p">}</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>On 64-bit targets, it will warn about truncating the 64-bit <code class=
=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">len</code> for the
32-bit parameter. To dismiss the warning, you must either address it by
using a loop to <a href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/blog/2023/02/13/">call <=
code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">WriteFile</code> multip=
le times</a>, or acknowledge the
truncation with an explicit cast and accept the consequences. In this case
I may know from context it=E2=80=99s impossible for the program to even con=
struct
such a large message, so I=E2=80=99d use an assertion and truncate.</p>

<div class=3D"language-c highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre c=
lass=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"kt">void</span> <span class=3D"nf">=
logmsg</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"kt">char</span> <span=
 class=3D"o">*</span><span class=3D"n">msg</span><span class=3D"p">,</span>=
 <span class=3D"kt">size_t</span> <span class=3D"n">len</span><span class=
=3D"p">)</span>
<span class=3D"p">{</span>
    <span class=3D"n">HANDLE</span> <span class=3D"n">err</span> <span clas=
s=3D"o">=3D</span> <span class=3D"n">GetStdHandle</span><span class=3D"p">(=
</span><span class=3D"n">STD_ERROR_HANDLE</span><span class=3D"p">);</span>
    <span class=3D"n">DWORD</span> <span class=3D"n">out</span><span class=
=3D"p">;</span>
    <span class=3D"n">assert</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D=
"n">len</span> <span class=3D"o">&lt;=3D</span> <span class=3D"mh">0xffffff=
ff</span><span class=3D"p">);</span>
    <span class=3D"n">WriteFile</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=
=3D"n">err</span><span class=3D"p">,</span> <span class=3D"n">msg</span><sp=
an class=3D"p">,</span> <span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">DWORD</=
span><span class=3D"p">)</span><span class=3D"n">len</span><span class=3D"p=
">,</span> <span class=3D"o">&amp;</span><span class=3D"n">out</span><span =
class=3D"p">,</span> <span class=3D"mi">0</span><span class=3D"p">);</span>
<span class=3D"p">}</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>You might consider changing the interface instead:</p>

<div class=3D"language-c highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre c=
lass=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"kt">void</span> <span class=3D"nf">=
logmsg</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"kt">char</span> <span=
 class=3D"o">*</span><span class=3D"n">msg</span><span class=3D"p">,</span>=
 <span class=3D"kt">uint32_t</span> <span class=3D"n">len</span><span class=
=3D"p">);</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>That probably passes the buck and doesn=E2=80=99t solve the underlying p=
roblem.
The caller may be holding a <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-r=
ouge">size_t</code> length, so the truncation happens
there instead. Or maybe you keep propagating this change backwards until
it, say, dissipates on a known constant. <code class=3D"language-plaintext =
highlighter-rouge">-=EF=BB=BFWconversion</code> leads to
these ripple effects that improves the overall program, which is why I
like it.</p>

<p>The catch is that the above warning only happens for 64-bit targets. So
you might miss it. The inverse is true in other cases. This is one area
where <a href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/blog/2021/08/21/">cross-architectu=
re testing</a> can pay off.</p>

<p>Unfortunately since this warning is off the beaten path, it seems like i=
t
doesn=E2=80=99t quite get the attention it could use. It warns about simple=
 cases
where truncation has been explicitly handled/avoided. For example:</p>

<div class=3D"language-c highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre c=
lass=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"kt">int</span> <span class=3D"n">x<=
/span> <span class=3D"o">=3D</span> <span class=3D"p">...;</span>
<span class=3D"kt">char</span> <span class=3D"n">digit</span> <span class=
=3D"o">=3D</span> <span class=3D"sc">'0'</span> <span class=3D"o">+</span> =
<span class=3D"n">x</span><span class=3D"o">%</span><span class=3D"mi">10</=
span><span class=3D"p">;</span>  <span class=3D"c1">// false warning</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>The <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">'0'</code> is a=
 known constant. The operation <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighte=
r-rouge">x%10</code> has a known range (-9
to 9). Therefore the addition result has a known range, and all results
can be represented in a <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge=
">char</code>. Yet it still warns. This often comes up
dealing with character data like this.</p>

<p>In my <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">logmsg</code>=
 fix I had used an assertion to check that no truncation
actually occurred. But wouldn=E2=80=99t it be nice if the compiler could ge=
nerate
that for us somehow? That brings us to dynamic checks.</p>

<h3 id=3D"dynamic-run-time-checks">Dynamic run-time checks</h3>

<p>Sanitizers have been around for nearly a decade but are still criminally
underused. They insert run-time assertions into programs at the flip of a
switch typically at a modest performance cost =E2=80=94 less than the cost =
of a
debug build. All three major compilers support at least one sanitizer on
all targets. In most cases, failing to use them is practically the same as
not even trying to find defects. Every beginner tutorial ought to be using
sanitizers <em>from page 1</em> where they teach how to compile a program w=
ith
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">gcc</code>. (That this=
 is universally <em>not</em> the case, and that these same
tutorials also do not begin with teaching a debugger, is a major, on-going
education failure.)</p>

<p>There are multiple different sanitizers with lots of overlap, but Addres=
s
Sanitizer (ASan) and Undefined Behavior Sanitizer (UBSan) are the most
general. They are compatible with each other and form a solid, general
baseline. To use address sanitizer, at both compile and link time do:</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>$ cc ... -fsanitize=3Daddress ...
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>It=E2=80=99s even spelled the same way in MSVC. It=E2=80=99s needed at l=
ink time because
it includes a runtime component. When working properly it=E2=80=99s aware o=
f all
allocations and checks all memory accesses that might be out of bounds,
producing a run-time error if that occurs. It=E2=80=99s not always appropri=
ate,
but most projects that can use it probably should.</p>

<p>UBSan is enabled similarly:</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>$ cc ... -fsanitize=3Dundefined ...
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>It adds checks around operations that might be undefined, emitting a
run-time error if it occurs. It has an optional runtime component to
produce a helpful diagnostic. You can instead insert a trap instruction,
which is how I prefer to use it: <strong><code class=3D"language-plaintext =
highlighter-rouge">-=EF=BB=BFfsanitize-trap=3Dundefined</code></strong>.
(Until recently it was <strong><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighte=
r-rouge">-=EF=BB=BFfsanitize-undefined-trap-on-error</code></strong>.)
This works on platforms where the UBSan runtime is unsupported. Some
instrumentation is only inserted at higher optimization levels.</p>

<p>For me, the most useful UBSan check is signed overflow =E2=80=94 e.g. co=
mputing
the wrong result =E2=80=94 and it=E2=80=99s instrumentation I miss when not=
 working in C.
In programs where this might be an issue, combine it <a href=3D"https://nul=
lprogram.com/blog/2019/01/25/">with a fuzzer</a>
to search for inputs that cause overflows. This is yet another argument in
favor of <a href=3D"https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/papers/201=
9/p1428r0.pdf">signed sizes</a>, as UBSan can detect such overflows. (Yes,
UBSan optionally instruments unsigned overflow, too, but then you must
somehow distinguish <a href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/blog/2019/11/19/">in=
tentional</a> from <a href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/blog/2017/07/19/">uni=
ntentional</a>
overflow.)</p>

<p>On Linux, ASan and UBSan strangely do not have <a href=3D"https://nullpr=
ogram.com/blog/2022/06/26/">debugger-oriented
defaults</a>. Fortunately that=E2=80=99s easy to address with a couple of
environment variables, which cause them to break on error instead of
uselessly exiting:</p>

<div class=3D"language-sh highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre =
class=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"nb">export </span><span class=3D"n=
v">ASAN_OPTIONS</span><span class=3D"o">=3D</span><span class=3D"nv">abort_=
on_error</span><span class=3D"o">=3D</span>1:halt_on_error<span class=3D"o"=
>=3D</span>1
<span class=3D"nb">export </span><span class=3D"nv">UBSAN_OPTIONS</span><sp=
an class=3D"o">=3D</span><span class=3D"nv">abort_on_error</span><span clas=
s=3D"o">=3D</span>1:halt_on_error<span class=3D"o">=3D</span>1
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Also, when compiling you can combine sanitizers like so:</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>$ cc ... -fsanitize=3Daddress,undefined ..=
.
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>As of this writing, MSVC does not have UBSan, but it does have a similar
feature, <a href=3D"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/cpp/build/reference/r=
tc-run-time-error-checks">run-time error checks</a>. Three sub-flags (<code=
 class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">c</code>, <code class=3D"la=
nguage-plaintext highlighter-rouge">s</code>, <code class=3D"language-plain=
text highlighter-rouge">u</code>)
enable different checks, and <strong><code class=3D"language-plaintext high=
lighter-rouge">/RTCcsu</code></strong> turns them all on. The <code class=
=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">c</code> flag
generates the assertion I had manually written with <code class=3D"language=
-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-=EF=BB=BFWconversion</code>,
and traps any truncation at run time. There=E2=80=99s nothing quite like th=
is in
UBSan! It=E2=80=99s so extreme that it=E2=80=99s compatible with neither st=
andard runtime
libraries (fortunately <a href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/blog/2023/02/11/"=
>not a big deal</a>) nor with ASan.</p>

<p>Caveat: Explicit casts aren=E2=80=99t enough, you must actually truncate=
 variables
using a mask in order to pass the check. For example, to accept truncation
in the <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">logmsg</code> f=
unction:</p>

<div class=3D"language-c highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre c=
lass=3D"highlight"><code>    <span class=3D"n">WriteFile</span><span class=
=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"n">err</span><span class=3D"p">,</span> <span=
 class=3D"n">msg</span><span class=3D"p">,</span> <span class=3D"n">len</sp=
an><span class=3D"o">&amp;</span><span class=3D"mh">0xffffffff</span><span =
class=3D"p">,</span> <span class=3D"o">&amp;</span><span class=3D"n">out</s=
pan><span class=3D"p">,</span> <span class=3D"mi">0</span><span class=3D"p"=
>);</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Thread Sanitizer (TSan) is occasionally useful for finding =E2=80=94 or,=
 more
often, <em>proving</em> the presence of =E2=80=94 data races. It has a runt=
ime component
and so must be used at compile time and link time.</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>$ cc ... -fsanitize=3Dthread ...
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Unfortunately it only works in a narrow context. The target must use
pthreads, not C11 threads, OpenMP, nor <a href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/b=
log/2023/03/23/">direct cloning</a>. It must
only synchronize through code that was compiled with TSan. That means no
synchronization <a href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/blog/2022/10/03/">throug=
h system calls</a>, especially no futexes. Most
non-trivial programs do not meet the criteria.</p>

<h3 id=3D"debug-information">Debug information</h3>

<p>Another common mistake in tutorials is using plain old <code class=3D"la=
nguage-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-=EF=BB=BFg</code> instead
of <strong><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-=EF=BB=BFg=
3</code></strong> (read: =E2=80=9Cdebug level 3=E2=80=9D). That=E2=80=99s l=
ike using <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-=EF=BB=BFO<=
/code>
instead of <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-=EF=BB=BFO=
3</code>. It adds a lot more debug information to the
output, particularly enums and macros. The extra information is useful and
you=E2=80=99re better off having it!</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>$ cc ... -g3 ...
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>All the major build systems =E2=80=94 CMake, Autotools, Meson, etc. =E2=
=80=94 get this
wrong in their standard debug configurations. Producing a fully-featured
debug build from these systems is a constant battle for me. Often it=E2=80=
=99s
easier to ignore the build system entirely and <code class=3D"language-plai=
ntext highlighter-rouge">cc -g3 **/*.c</code> (plus
sanitizers, etc.).</p>

<p>(Short term note: GCC 11, released in March 2021, switched to DWARF5 by
default. However, GDB could not access the extra <code class=3D"language-pl=
aintext highlighter-rouge">-=EF=BB=BFg3</code> debug
information in DWARF5 until GDB 13, released February 2023. If you have a
toolchain from that two year window =E2=80=94 except <a href=3D"https://git=
hub.com/skeeto/w64devkit">mine</a> because I patched
it =E2=80=94 then you may also need <code class=3D"language-plaintext highl=
ighter-rouge">-=EF=BB=BFgdwarf-4</code> to switch back to DWARF4.)</p>

<p>What about <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-=EF=BB=
=BFOg</code>? In theory it enables optimizations that do not
interfere with debugging, and potentially some additional warnings. In
practice I still get far too many =E2=80=9Coptimized out=E2=80=9D messages =
from GDB when I
use it, so I don=E2=80=99t bother. Fortunately C is such a simple language =
that
debug builds are nearly as fast as release builds anyway.</p>

<p>On MSVC I like having debug information embedded in binaries, as GCC doe=
s,
which is done using <strong><code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-r=
ouge">/Z7</code></strong>.</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>$ cl ... /Z7 ...
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Though I certainly understand the value of separate debug information,
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">/Zi</code>, in some ca=
ses. Sometimes I wish the GNU toolchain made this easier.</p>

<h3 id=3D"summary">Summary</h3>

<p>My personal rigorous baseline for development using <code class=3D"langu=
age-plaintext highlighter-rouge">gcc</code> and <code class=3D"language-pla=
intext highlighter-rouge">clang</code>
looks like this (all platforms):</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>$ cc -g3 -Wall -Wextra -Wconversion -Wdoub=
le-promotion
     -Wno-unused-parameter -Wno-unused-function -Wno-sign-conversion
     -fsanitize=3Dundefined -fsanitize-trap ...
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>While ASan is great for quickly reviewing and evaluating other people=E2=
=80=99s
projects, I don=E2=80=99t find it useful for my own programs. I avoid that =
class
of defects through smarter paradigms (region-based allocation, no null
terminated strings, etc.). I also prefer the behavior of trap instruction
UBSan versus a diagnostic, as it behaves better under debuggers.</p>

<p>For <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">cl</code> and <=
code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">clang-cl</code>, my per=
sonal baseline looks like this:</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>$ cl /Z7 /W4 /wd4146 /wd4245 /RTCcsu ...
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>I don=E2=80=99t normally need <code class=3D"language-plaintext highligh=
ter-rouge">/D_CRT_SECURE_NO_WARNINGS</code> since I don=E2=80=99t use a CRT
anyway.</p>

<p><strong>Update</strong>: Peter0x44 points out <code class=3D"language-pl=
aintext highlighter-rouge">-D_GLIBCXX_DEBUG</code> if you=E2=80=99re workin=
g in
C++ with libstdc++, including on Windows with Mingw-w64. I agree, this is
an excellent option! ASan does not =E2=80=9Csee=E2=80=9D C++ containers, an=
d it fills in
some of those gaps.</p>



